---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const tsCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '50 + 10*sin(2*pi*x/12) + 0.8*x', label: 'level + seasonality + trend', color: '#374151', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '50 + 0.8*x', label: 'the underlying trend', color: 'rgba(180,35,24,0.7)', width: 1.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 48, label: 'months' },
  y: { min: 40, max: 110, label: 'sales (schematic)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/advanced/time-series-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Time-series data breaks ordinary statistics' rules: the points are <strong>ordered and
    correlated with their own past</strong>. Yesterday's temperature predicts today's; this
    month's sales carry last year's echo. Analysis therefore decomposes series into
    <strong>trend + seasonality + noise</strong>, and models the dependence explicitly — which is
    why forecasting is its own craft, not regression with dates.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the classic decomposition — a rising trend, a yearly wave, and the residual noise between them" widget="plotter" config={tsCfg} />

  <p>
    Strip the seasonal wave and the straight trend from the wiggly line and what remains is the
    noise you can model. Most "surprising" business patterns dissolve into one of these three
    components — or into a comparison error (this quarter vs last, instead of this quarter vs
    <em>this quarter last year</em>).
  </p>

  <h2>The model zoo</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Random walk:</strong> tomorrow = today + noise. Stock prices are modelled this way for good reasons — past direction carries no information (the efficient-market claim).</li>
    <li><strong>ARIMA:</strong> autoregressive + integrated + moving-average terms — the classical forecast engine behind demand planning for fifty years.</li>
    <li><strong>Exponential smoothing:</strong> weight recent points more; the Holt-Winters variant handles trend and seasonality and still beats fancier methods on many business series.</li>
    <li><strong>Stationarity matters:</strong> models assume stable statistical behaviour over time. Differencing (analysing changes, not levels) restores it — the "I" in ARIMA.</li>
    <li><strong>Spurious regression:</strong> two trending series correlate strongly with no connection (ice-cream sales and drownings, both rising). Detrending is the cure; scepticism the vaccine.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Box & Jenkins, Time Series Analysis (1970); Hyndman & Athanasopoulos, Forecasting: Principles and Practice (free).</p>
</TopicPage>
